    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Swanson Senior Muscle Retention Protein is a decent 25 g whey blend with no added sugar, but it is expensive and not independently certified: the 10-serving tub costs about $3.44 to $3.48 per 25 g of protein at Amazon prices. The wheat and soy on its allergen line, and your health conditions, change the answer. This review covers the label, testing, cost, buyer feedback and safer alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Swanson Senior Muscle Retention Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Swanson Senior Muscle Retention Protein is a reasonable whey-based shake for older adults, but it is one of the most expensive ways to buy 25 g of protein and it carries no independent certification seal. Per <a href="https://www.swansonvitamins.com/p/swanson-ultra-senior-muscle-retention-protein-powder-chocolate-1-06-lb-480-grams-pwdr">Swanson's label</a> at the time of writing, two scoops give 25 g of protein, 180 calories and 0 g of added sugar, and the 1.06 lb tub holds only 10 servings, which is about $3.44 to $3.48 per 25 g of protein at the Amazon prices we saw.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you want a stevia-sweetened, no-added-sugar shake with a little fiber and whole grains in the scoop, you do not need it to be gluten-free, and price is not your main concern. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you have celiac disease or a wheat allergy, you need a product verified by NSF or Informed Choice, you are on a kidney diet, or you simply want the most protein per dollar. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-seniors/">best protein powder for seniors</a> guide ranks cheaper and better-tested options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Swanson Senior Muscle Retention, per the label</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">25 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein per 2 scoops</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">180</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">10</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Servings per tub</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$3.44</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 25 g protein (Amazon)</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Both Swanson Senior Flavors and Three Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The two Swanson Senior Muscle Retention tubs sold on Amazon, Swanson's own cheaper grass-fed whey, a gluten-free whey isolate and the senior shake most doctors' offices mention. Prices are the Amazon prices we saw on November 18, 2026, and they move often.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Swanson Senior Muscle Retention Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Swanson Senior Muscle Retention Protein is mostly whey protein concentrate, with small amounts of pea protein, whole goat milk, an organic six-grain blend, soluble fiber and MCT oil. Swanson's typical-analysis panel lists about 29.9 g of 80% whey concentrate per serving, 5.2 g of ModCarb organic grains (oat bran, amaranth, quinoa, buckwheat, millet and chia), 2.6 g of pea protein, 2 g of goat milk, Fibersol-2 soluble fiber and 1.3 to 1.7 g of an MCT blend, per the <a href="https://www.swansonvitamins.com/p/swanson-ultra-senior-muscle-retention-protein-powder-natural-vanilla-1-06-lb-480-grams-pwdr">vanilla</a> and chocolate product pages at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The nutrition panel is the same for both flavors on the headline numbers: 180 calories, 25 g of protein, 3.5 g of fat, 11 g of carbohydrate, 3.5 g of fiber, 2.5 g of total sugar and 0 g of added sugar per 48 g serving. Sodium is 120 mg. Chocolate lists 310 mg of potassium and vanilla 175 mg, and both list 150 mg of phosphorus and 133 mg of calcium. The minerals are mostly modest, apart from selenium (44% of the daily value) and molybdenum (80%).
          </p>

          <p>
            Two lines deserve attention. All 3.5 g of fat is saturated, which is 18% of the daily value, and there is 60 mg of cholesterol, 20% of the daily value. That comes from the whey, goat milk and MCT oil, and it is higher than a whey isolate would give you. For most people it is a small share of the day, but anyone on a cholesterol-lowering diet should count it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What sweetener does it use, and is there added leucine or vitamin D?</h3>

          <p>
            The only sweetener is rebaudioside A, a purified stevia extract, so there is no sucralose, acesulfame potassium or sugar alcohol. There is <strong>no added leucine and no vitamin D</strong> on either label at the time of writing, and Swanson does not publish an amino acid profile. That matters because the name promises muscle retention, and leucine and vitamin D are the two nutrients senior-specific formulas usually lean on. Whey is naturally rich in leucine, so 25 g of whey still supplies a useful amount, but the formula adds nothing beyond what any whey shake already gives you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Swanson markets the powder as Non-GMO and vegetarian, and says the whey comes from grass-fed, hormone-free cows. It also shows a badge for clinically studied ingredients without naming the studies on the product page. The page now pitches the shake as a companion for people on GLP-1 medications, with a suggestion to spread 25 to 40 g of protein every three to four hours. We cover that use separately in our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-glp-1/">protein powder for GLP-1 users</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is Swanson Senior Muscle Retention Protein Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Swanson says it uses third-party testing, but the Senior Muscle Retention powder carries no independent certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice. On its <a href="https://www.swansonvitamins.com/pages/quality">quality page</a>, Swanson says it uses "third-party pharmaceutical-grade testing to ensure purity, potency, and reliability," that its facility is cGMP certified by NSF, and that all products are tested in GMP-certified facilities. Neither product page mentions testing beyond those company-wide statements at the time of writing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those claims are not meaningless. An NSF GMP facility registration means an auditor checks the manufacturing system, and testing finished products against label claims is the right practice. But a GMP audit certifies the facility, not the product in the tub, and it does not involve screening each lot for banned substances or publishing results. Swanson does not offer a batch Certificate of Analysis lookup for this product, and we found no heavy-metal results published for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most older adults, banned-substance screening is not the point. The questions that matter more are whether the protein content matches the label and whether lead or cadmium levels are low. On both, you are trusting Swanson's internal program rather than a public database. If you want a product a certifier has listed by name, the Dymatize ISO100 card above is the closest swap, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-seniors/">senior protein powder</a> rankings flag the certified options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A certified facility is not a certified product</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">NSF GMP registration audits how a factory works. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Choice test the product itself and list it by name in a public database. Swanson Senior Muscle Retention has the first, per Swanson, and not the second.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Does Swanson Senior Protein Cost per 25 g of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Swanson Senior Muscle Retention Protein costs about $3.44 per 25 g of protein in chocolate and about $3.48 in vanilla at the Amazon prices we saw on November 18, 2026. The math is simple because one serving is exactly 25 g of protein: the $34.39 chocolate tub divided by 10 servings is $3.44, and the $34.79 vanilla tub divided by 10 is $3.48. Swanson's own site listed chocolate at the same $34.39 one-time price, or about $3.44 a serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is expensive. Mainstream whey usually lands between about $1.20 and $2.20 per 25 g, and even Swanson's own grass-fed whey worked out to about $2.29 per 25 g on the day we checked. A daily Swanson Senior shake costs roughly $103 a month; the same 25 g from Ensure Max Protein would be about $58, and from Dymatize ISO100 about $64.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
