          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the Senior Label Means a Special Formula</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Apart from a grain blend, soluble fiber and a little goat milk, this is a whey concentrate shake. There is no added leucine, vitamin D or HMB on the label at the time of writing. A plain whey at half the price delivers the same 25 g of muscle-building protein, so judge it on the label, not the name.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying It for a Gluten-Free Diet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Swanson's allergen statement lists wheat, and the chocolate page spells out gluten, even though the named grains look gluten-free at a glance. Anyone with celiac disease or a wheat allergy should skip it and choose a powder labeled gluten-free, such as a whey isolate or the Ensure Max Protein shake.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Tub Prices Instead of Cost per 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $34 tub looks similar to many whey tubs until you notice it holds only 10 servings. At about $3.44 per 25 g it costs more than double some alternatives. Always divide the price by total grams of protein in the container, then multiply by 25, before deciding what is good value.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding Protein Without Checking a Kidney Diet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Older adults with chronic kidney disease are often given a daily protein, potassium and phosphorus limit. One shake adds 25 g of protein, 150 mg of phosphorus and up to 310 mg of potassium. Ask your nephrologist or renal dietitian how it fits before adding it, rather than finding out at the next blood test.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting the Shake to Replace Strength Training</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein supplements help preserve muscle mainly when paired with resistance exercise. Drinking a shake every morning while staying inactive will close a nutrition gap but will not rebuild lost strength. Two or three short sessions a week with bands, chair stands or light weights make every gram count.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

